{
  "duration": 129.99000549316406,
  "language": "english",
  "text": "Also, it's passed a bunch of laws addressing these other kinds of risks from AI. I think this is a recognition that AI is affecting people in a lot of ways and that there are a lot of things that the government can and probably should do to help mitigate those risks in terms of how they affect the citizenry. Yeah, definitely. I think it just goes to show it's not an either-or question when it comes to regulating AI. We see sometimes people in the policy space say, these catastrophic risks are just a distraction from the more mundane, the concrete risks that we're already seeing manifest. And this just seems like evidence that you don't have to just go after one or the other. You can set up guardrails around those more concrete risks around child safety, et cetera, while also moving the needle on frontier AI systems. Yeah. And I think that that's a good place to end the podcast. We've covered a lot. I think the AI agents didn't realize that we were supposed to have an August recess. So hopefully they've learned their lesson and will give us a little bit of a break before we come back in two weeks. Yes. Fingers crossed. Although I'm not feeling super optimistic given how things are playing out, but one can hope. Okay. Thanks, Alok. And we'll see our audience next time. See you in two weeks. Thanks for listening to this episode of the AI Policy Podcast. If you enjoyed the show, consider leaving us a five-star review on your favorite podcast platform. We'd also love your feedback on the show. Please email us at AIPolicyPodcast at CSIS.org. And don't forget to visit our website, CSIS.org, for the Wadwani AI Center's latest research and events. This podcast was produced by Sarah Baker and Nicole Herrera. See you next week. ",
